+\subsection{Captions and \cs{caption*}}
+
+\LaTeX{} provides \cs{caption*} for captions without a number (see
+\texttt{usrguide}). It uses the class's \cs{@makecaption}, and the kernel
+offers hooks and sockets so that classes and packages do not need to
+redefine \cs{caption} or \cs{@caption} (see the documentation of
+\texttt{ltfloat.dtx}). For classes this means:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \cs{@makecaption}\arg{label}\arg{text} is also used for
+  \cs{caption*}. Then the switch \cs{if@captionstar} is true and
+  \meta{label} is \cs{@caption@nolabel}. This label is not empty (a test
+  for an empty label does not work), but it typesets nothing, and it
+  removes the usual separators that follow it directly: a colon or a
+  period and the space after them, also after the end of a group or of
+  \cs{textbf}. So a \cs{@makecaption} that typesets \verb|#1: #2|, as the
+  standard classes do, or \verb|{\bfseries #1.} #2| and similar forms
+  needs no change. A class whose separator is in a macro, or that adds
+  other space or characters after the label, should omit the label
+  \emph{and} the separator if the switch is true. Otherwise they are
+  printed before the text; in most cases \LaTeX{} then warns once, but
+  not always (for example not for a separator after a group that
+  contains only the label, as in \verb|\textit{#1}\textbf{:}|). The
+  switch is the only test that classes should use, for example:
+\begin{verbatim}
+\sbox\@tempboxa{\if@captionstar\else#1\captionsep\fi#2}%
+\end{verbatim}
+  Test the switch directly (not with \cs{ifx} against \cs{iftrue}, which
+  would unbalance a conditional when the code is in a skipped branch). A
+  class that wants to work with older formats can provide the switch
+  first:
+\begin{verbatim}
+\@ifundefined{if@captionstar}{\newif\if@captionstar}{}
+\end{verbatim}
+\item A class that redefines \cs{@caption} (for example to change the
+  list entry or the spacing) should preferably use the hooks and sockets
+  of the caption interface instead (hooks \texttt{caption/before},
+  \texttt{caption/prepare}; sockets \texttt{caption/listentry},
+  \texttt{caption/typeset}). An
+  existing redefinition that typesets the label with
+  \cs{fnum@}\meta{type}, writes the list entry with
+  \verb|\addcontentsline{\csname ext@|\meta{type}\verb|\endcsname}|
+  and makes a link target from \cs{theH}\meta{type} (if at all), as the
+  kernel and \pkg{hyperref} did before, gives a correct \cs{caption*}
+  without changes: for \cs{caption*} the kernel lets
+  \cs{fnum@}\meta{type} be the label described above, makes
+  \cs{ext@}\meta{type} empty and gives \cs{theH}\meta{type} a unique
+  suffix, until the next \cs{caption} or the end of the group (so also if
+  the redefinition defers its work). For a caption that does not step
+  its counter (a continued float) only the unique suffix is used. A
+  redefinition can also test \cs{if@captionstar} itself, and one that
+  defers its work should do so before it defers. As the switch also
+  stays true until the next \cs{caption} or the end of the group, a
+  redefinition that tests it (or whose \cs{@makecaption} does) should
+  set it false at its end (\cs{@captionstarfalse}); otherwise a later
+  \cs{@makecaption} in the same float, for example for a listing, would
+  omit its label as well.
+\item A class or package that redefines \cs{caption} itself takes over
+  the star as well: unless it handles it (no counter step, no list entry,
+  \cs{@caption@nolabel} as label for \cs{@makecaption} with
+  \cs{if@captionstar} true),
+  \verb|\caption*{Text}| gives a numbered caption with the text `*' and a
+  list entry, followed by `Text'. It is better to keep the kernel
+  definition and to use the hooks and sockets. Code that keeps its own
+  \cs{caption} and passes only \cs{caption*} to the kernel must start its
+  own numbered \cs{caption} with \cs{@kernel@caption@reset}.
+\end{itemize}
+
 
