+% DRAFT (proposal (b) v2): publish this section only together with
+% support in hyperref (hyperref should not replace \caption on kernels
+% with the caption interface, i.e., \hyper@nopatch@caption when
+% \@kernel@caption is defined). Until then \caption* gives the old output
+% with hyperref without \DocumentMetadata, the most common setup.
+\section{Captions without a number}
+
+\begin{decl}
+  |\caption*| \oarg{opt} \arg{text}
+\end{decl}
+Where \cs{caption} can be used (inside a float, for example), \cs{caption*}
+typesets \meta{text} as a caption without a label: the counter of the float
+is not stepped, nothing is written to the list of figures, tables, etc., and
+no link target is made. There is nothing for a \cs{label} to refer to, so do
+not use \cs{label} after \cs{caption*} (as after \cs{section*}, it would
+record whatever was set last). The optional argument is accepted for symmetry with
+\cs{caption}; the kernel does not use it, but packages may.
+
+The caption has the layout of the document class. The standard classes and
+many others omit the label and its separator (a colon or a period). A class
+or package whose separator is defined in another way and that has not yet
+been updated for \cs{caption*} prints that separator before the text (for
+example `: Text' with \pkg{KOMA-Script} and \pkg{memoir}, `. Text' with the
+AMS classes, and `-- Text' with \pkg{babel-french} for French and
+pdf\TeX); in most cases \LaTeX{} then warns once. It still gives no number and no list
+entry. Packages
+and classes that replace \cs{caption} itself (at present, for example,
+\pkg{hyperref} without \cs{DocumentMetadata}, also when a class loads it,
+\pkg{beamer}, and some journal and thesis classes) do not know
+\cs{caption*} yet: with them \verb|\caption*{Text}| gives, as before, a
+numbered caption with the text `*' and a list entry, followed by `Text'.
+With \pkg{float} (without \pkg{hyperref}, or with \cs{DocumentMetadata})
+the kernel definition is used, also for floats restyled by \pkg{float},
+which show only their last caption. The \pkg{captcont}
+package has a \cs{caption*} of its own, which gives a numbered caption
+without list entry. The \pkg{caption} package supports \cs{caption*}
+with \pkg{hyperref}, \pkg{beamer} and \pkg{float}, but not with every
+class that replaces \cs{caption} (with some, \cs{caption*} still prints
+a label with a number).
+
 \section{Support for problem solving}
 
